Your rules for this feature, as the builders will follow them (each one applies to every case like it, not just the example):
  1. Whenever a refund is calculated, the item refund amount is what the customer actually paid for the returned items: each item's price times its quantity, minus any discount code applied, not the list price. For example, a dress listed at ₹3,000 bought with a 20% coupon for ₹2,400 has an item refund amount of ₹2,400.
  2. Whenever a refund amount (for example after a fee or a part refund) comes out with paise, round it down to whole rupees, and never refund more than the customer paid. For example, ₹1,349.60 becomes ₹1,349.
  3. Whenever an order is returned for a full refund, the customer keeps the loyalty points that order earned.
  4. Whenever an item is returned because it was damaged and the return is made within 90 days of delivery, refund the full item refund amount plus the shipping fee.
  5. Whenever an item is returned for any reason other than damage, charge no change-of-mind fee and do not refund the shipping fee. If the return is made within 14 days of delivery, refund the full item refund amount. If it is made on day 15 through day 30 after delivery, refund half the item refund amount. If it is made after day 30, refund nothing.
  6. Whenever a refund is issued, whatever the reason or timing, it goes back through the payment method the customer originally used: card payments are refunded to the card, and store-credit payments are refunded as store credit.
  7. Whenever a refund request's reason is exactly "damaged", apply the damaged-goods rules. For any other reason, including an unrecognised one like "too big" or no reason at all, apply the standard 14-day and 30-day return rules.
  8. Whenever an order earns loyalty points, it earns 1 point for every full ₹100 of its item total after discounts, not counting shipping. For example, ₹1,250 earns 12 points. An order paid with store credit earns no points, and so does an order whose items come to less than ₹500 after discounts. A final-sale order earns double points. Each order is paid either by card or with store credit.
  9. Whenever the daily summary counts orders, it counts every order in the list it is given, whatever its status (paid, cancelled, refunded, awaiting payment, or paid with store credit), with no filtering by date or time.
  10. Whenever daily revenue is calculated, it is the sum over card orders only of each order's item total after discounts plus its shipping. Refunds are not subtracted, store-credit orders add nothing, and tax gets no separate handling.
  11. Whenever average order value is shown, it equals total card-order revenue divided by the number of card orders, rounded down to whole rupees. For example, ₹10,000 from 3 card orders shows ₹3,333. On a day with no card orders it shows ₹0.
